Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Thermocouples", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.949231265748098*^9, 
  3.949231269149295*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7bc0375d-4ed3-4089-a34e-\
cb8a0999ec5f"],

Cell["\<\
It was noticed by the German physicist Thomas Seebeck in 1821  that when two \
dissimilar metals are welded or clamped together that a small voltage is \
generated which increases nonlinearly with temperature. This is the principle \
by which thermocouples work (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermocouple ).

Thermocouples are one of the most commonly used thermal sensors. There are \
many different types of thermocouples. Some are much more sensitive (mV/\
\[Degree]C) than others, and so are suitable for very low temperatures and \
others for very high temperatures. Type K (consisting of the alloys chromel \
and alumel) and Type J (consisting of iron and the alloy constantan) are the \
most commonly used types.

Without calibration it is difficult to get temperature measurements to much \
better than \[PlusMinus]1 \[Degree]C. 

The typical measurement configuration is shown in the figure below (which is \
taken from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermocouple ):\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.949231278316289*^9, 3.949231497655829*^9}, {
  3.9492315372444487`*^9, 3.9492315724944267`*^9}, {3.949232680088209*^9, 
  3.949232949178334*^9}, {3.949232999087315*^9, 3.9492330111470747`*^9}, {
