  RowBox[{"1.9284391386206358`*^-18", " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["t", "7"]}], "-", 
  RowBox[{"1.5621096518375274`*^-21", " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["t", "8"]}], "+", 
  RowBox[{"6.337253721248624`*^-25", " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["t", "9"]}], "-", 
  RowBox[{"1.0366111971948699`*^-28", " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["t", "10"]}]}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.949329364327241*^9, 3.981451521682557*^9, 
  3.9814519242310658`*^9, 3.98275587645632*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[70]=",ExpressionUUID->"b2801964-72f5-1947-853e-9173d56eaa1c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Notice that we have very small numbers multiplying the temperature, t, raise \
to very high powers. This can lead to numerical problems particularly of the \
processor undertaking the calculation, such as a microcontroller, is using \
single precision floating point numbers.
A better strategy is to fit rational polynomials. If the objective is to \
minimize that maximum error (as opposed to the average error squared) then \
rational Chebyshev polynomials should be considered. Minimizing the maximum \
error called MinMax parameter estimation.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9493294262345533`*^9, 3.9493296885209703`*^9}, {
  3.949329733566391*^9, 
  3.9493297841351852`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5c3b15cf-2674-4d9b-9e64-\
3a04b184e916"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Interpolating Polynomials", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.949329910709161*^9, 3.949329926875299*^9}, {
  3.9493299685488977`*^9, 
  3.949329971881179*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d1fa4e1f-9138-4da2-a3e2-\
a44ccce94f7b"],

Cell["\<\
Instead of fitting a single high order polynomial we can fit what are called \
piece-wise interpolation functions. Such functions pass through all the data \
points and so the residuals should be 0:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.94932997801481*^9, 3.949329991066142*^9}, {
  3.949330170770879*^9, 3.949330243127942*^9}, {3.9493412264021053`*^9, 
  3.949341237080014*^9}, {3.949341307715991*^9, 3.9493413119626093`*^9}, {
  3.9493413470339003`*^9, 
  3.94934138399471*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d4bc4b2d-0dfb-4e97-b565-\
785f311665bd"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

